<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Dance · c. 1399 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Llibre Vermell de Montserrat</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A book written around 1399 with songs and circle dances for pilgrims visiting a mountain monastery in Catalonia.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Pilgrims to the shrine of the Virgin of Montserrat, Catalonia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Catalan, Occitan, Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Monastery of Montserrat, near Barcelona, Catalonia, Spain</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>manuscript prepared c. 1399; some music older in style</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous songs, manuscript c. 1399)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llibre-vermell-de-montserrat">Llibre Vermell de Montserrat</a>, the &quot;Red Book of Montserrat&quot;, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> collection of devotional texts that includes a small group of late medieval songs. It was prepared in approximately 1399 at the monastery of Montserrat outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, in Catalonia, where it is still kept. The monastery held the shrine of the Virgin of Montserrat, a major site of pilgrimage at the time. The manuscript originally had 172 double pages, of which 32 have been lost, and six folios contain music. Its name comes from the red binding in which it was placed in the 19th century. No composer is named for any of its songs.</p><p>The anonymous compiler explained in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> note why the songs were written down. Pilgrims keeping their night watch in the church of the Blessed Mary of Montserrat, and in the square by day, wished to sing and dance; since only chaste and pious songs were fit for the church, these were set down for them, to be used modestly so as not to disturb those at prayer. The songs were thus meant for singing and for circle dances.</p><p>Ten songs survive, in Catalan, Occitan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, most of them, by their titles, addressed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>; the last, Ad Mortem Festinamus (&quot;We hasten towards death&quot;), is a virelai on death. They include virelais, a ballad-dance, songs and a motet. Some are monophonic and can be sung as two- or threefold canons; others are set in two to four parts, usually in non-imitative polyphony. They combine features of folk song and hymn.</p><p>The book was written near the end of the 14th century, but much of its music appears from its style to be older. Among them is the motet Imperayritz de la ciutat joyosa / Verges ses par misericordiosa (&quot;Empress of the happy city&quot; / &quot;Virgin, out of mercy&quot;), whose two texts are sung at the same time, a manner that would already have been old-fashioned when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was compiled.</p><p>Their simple forms, dance rhythms and strong melodies have made these songs some of the most frequently recorded pieces of early music. Recordings began with Atrium Musicae in 1971 and include Jordi Savall with Hespèrion XX (1979) and with La Capella Reial de Catalunya and Hespèrion XXI (2016), as well as Micrologus, Studio der Frühen Musik and Ensemble Unicorn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A pilgrims' songbook: judging by their titles, most of the songs praise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, calling her a star, a queen of heaven and a virgin mother, and invite the singers to join together in her praise. One song, Ad Mortem Festinamus, turns to death and the shortness of life. The book's own note says they were meant to keep night vigils and daytime dancing devout.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin and Catalan</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin and Catalan</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><p class="sg-sthead">Stella splendens</p><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sequitur alia cantilena ad trepudium rotundum:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There follows another song, for a round dance:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Stella splendens in monte ut solis radium miraculis serrato exaudi populum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Star shining on the mountain like a sunbeam, on the saw-toothed mountain of miracles, hear the people.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Concurrunt universi gaudentes populi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All the peoples run together rejoicing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">divites et egeni grandes et parvuli</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>rich and poor, great and small;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ipsum ingrediuntur ut cernunt oculi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they climb it so that their eyes may see,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et inde revertuntur gracijis repleti.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and return from it filled with graces.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Principes et magnates extirpe regia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Princes and magnates of royal stock,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">saeculi potestates obtenta venia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the powers of the age, having obtained pardon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">peccaminum proclamant tundentes pectora</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>confess their sins, beating their breasts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">poplite flexo clamant hic: Ave Maria.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and on bended knee cry here: Ave Maria.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Prelati et barones comites incliti</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Prelates and barons, famous counts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">religiosi omnes atque presbyteri</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>all the religious and the priests,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">milites mercatores cives marinari</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>soldiers, merchants, citizens, sailors,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">burgenses piscatores praemiantur ibi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>townsmen, fishermen, are rewarded there.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Rustici aratores nec non notarii</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Peasants, ploughmen, and also notaries,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">advocati scultores cuncti ligni</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>advocates, sculptors, all the wood-</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fabri sartores et sutores nec non lanifici</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>workers, tailors and cobblers, and also wool-workers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">artifices et omnes gratulantur ibi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>craftsmen and all give thanks there.</span></p></div><p class="sg-sthead">Polorum regina</p><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">A ball redon:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For a round dance:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Polorum regina omnium nostra. Stella matutina dele scelera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Queen of all the heavens, our morning star, blot out our sins.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ante partum virgo Deo gravida Semper permansisti inviolata.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Before the birth a virgin, pregnant by God, you remained ever inviolate.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et in partu virgo Deo fecunda Semper permansisti inviolata.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And in the birth a virgin, fruitful by God, you remained ever inviolate.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et post partum virgo mater enixa Semper permansisti inviolata.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And after the birth a virgin, a mother delivered, you remained ever inviolate.</span></p></div><p class="sg-sthead">Cuncti simus concanentes</p><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">A ball redon:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For a round dance:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Cuncti simus concanentes Ave Maria.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us all sing together: Ave Maria.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Virgo sola existente en affuit angelus Gabriel est appellatus atque missus celitus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>While the Virgin was alone, behold, an angel came; he is called Gabriel and was sent from heaven.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Clara facieque dixit: Ave Maria. Clara facieque dixit: audite karissimi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With bright face he said: Ave Maria. With bright face he said: hear, dearest ones.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">En concipies Maria Pariesque filium audite karissimi Vocabis eum Ihesum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Behold, you shall conceive, Mary, and bear a son, hear, dearest ones; you shall call him Jesus.</span></p></div><p class="sg-sthead">Los set gotxs recomptarem (the opening)</p><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ballada dels goytxs de Nostre Dona en vulgar cathallan a ball redon:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Song of the joys of Our Lady in the Catalan vernacular, for a round dance:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Los set gotxs recomptarem et devotament xantant</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall recount the seven joys, and singing devoutly</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">humilment saludarem la dolça verge Maria.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we shall humbly greet the sweet Virgin Mary.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ave Maria gracia plena Dominus tecum Virgo serena.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail Mary, full of grace, the Lord is with you, serene Virgin.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Llibre Vermell de Montserrat (c. 1399): Stella splendens, Polorum regina, Cuncti simus concanentes and the opening of Los set gotxs, each with the manuscript's rubric; as quoted in the Spanish and Catalan Wikipedias' articles. Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llibre_Vermell_de_Montserrat" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Llibre Vermell de Montserrat</a>, <a href="https://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llibre_vermell_de_Montserrat" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Llibre vermell de Montserrat</a>); public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>Four of the manuscript's ten songs: the first stanzas of Stella splendens, and Polorum regina, Cuncti simus concanentes and the opening of Los set gotxs, the one in Catalan. Each opens with the manuscript's rubric, telling how it is to be danced (a ball redon, ad trepudium rotundum: as a round dance).</li><li>Stella splendens: the &quot;saw-toothed&quot; mountain (serratus) is a play on the name Montserrat, the &quot;sawn mountain&quot;; the song lists pilgrims of every rank, which is why it is often read as a picture of medieval society.</li><li>Stella splendens, line 5.2: the source breaks &quot;ligni fabri&quot; (wood-workers, carpenters) across two lines, and the English follows.</li><li>The manuscript says these songs were written so that pilgrims keeping vigil in the church could sing and dance decently, since they would sing and dance anyway.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="Eq8dH3LtA_Q" data-title="Llibre Vermell de Montserrat: Ensemble Apotropaïk in concert (France Musique)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Ensemble Apotropaïk in concert (France Musique)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel France Musique concerts. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Eq8dH3LtA_Q" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Futuro antico</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1996</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Angelo Branduardi recorded &quot;Los set goyts&quot; and &quot;Imperayritz de la ciutat joyosa&quot; on the album</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lord of the Flies (film score by Philippe Sarde)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melodies of &quot;Stella Splendens&quot;, &quot;Los Set Gotxs&quot; and &quot;Mariam Matrem&quot; used, uncredited, in original orchestrations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Llibre Vermell de Montserrat: A fourteenth century pilgrimage (Hespèrion XX, Jordi Savall)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1979</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li></ul></section>
